  "slug": "decoding-pathology-17b5",
  "summary": "New preprints decode H&E into molecular and senescence states and separate heritable from niche effects on tumor clones in spatial transcriptomics.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational recovery of single-cell molecular state from tissue measurements. Three preprints treat H&E as a molecular readout. Loki2, trained on 15 million cells, tests how much molecular and cellular state routine histology encodes. GenVinci reconstructs 3D molecular tissue scenes and maps senescence across aging mouse organs and human skin. sc-HistoMorphomics links 194 nuclear morphometric features to spatial transcriptomics. A newer strand integrates lineage with space. SpOTr uses phylogeny-aware Fused Gromov-Wasserstein transport to place tumor clones onto spots, and SOUR separates heritable from microenvironmental contributions to expression in spatial lineage tracing data. CIRCA recovers identifiable cell and niche programs tied to plaque pathology. Representation learning continues: MCT models cell sets to learn gene co-regulation, and UCE embeddings of 59M+ cells across 614 states map state bridges. Dynamics and integration methods (DRAFT, GatorAnchor) and feedback-network models of heterogeneity restoration form a secondary strand.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
